    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Clinical data have demonstrated that on average, diabetic patients have abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA) that grow more slowly and rupture less often than non-diabetic patients. What has not been elucidated is if this protective quality is due to diabetes itself or the common drug used to treat diabetes, metformin. Metformin has been shown to work as an anti-inflammatory, decrease MMP activity, and deactivate macrophages. This study used a murine model of human AAAs and noninvasive imaging and histopathological analyses to investigate the impact of metformin on the formation and progression of AAAs. The mice were split into two groups: one was treated with metformin and the other given vehicle (water). Ultrasound imaging showed that the treatment group had aneurysms that grew significantly more slowly. Histological analysis of the aortic tissue revealed that the treatment group had significantly decreased neoangiogenesis and macrophage accumulation when compared to the control group. My research hopes to inform future studies into the mechanism of aneurysm formation and potential drug therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
